  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nRain continues into this evening\n\nAs expected, rain is ongoing across the region early this afternoon \nahead of a southern stream wave currently moving over NE/KS. Rain \namounts have been light, generally around less than 0.5\" over the \npast 6 hours. Expect these rain rates to continue through the \nevening so some spots could get up to 1.5\", including morning rain, \nbut most will end up with 0.5-1\" as their final totals.\n\nBreezy with spotty showers for Friday\n\nFriday, after the southern stream wave phases with the northern \nstream trough currently over Lake Winnipeg, surface low to our east \nwill deepen considerably and temperatures aloft in our area cool \nrapidly. A strong surface gradient will be present with pressure \nrises near 2 mb/hr and a northerly 30-40 knot 850mb jet will move \nover the area. The end result will be breezy conditions with \npotential (40-60% per 15.13z NBM) for a 45 mph gust, particularly \nwest of the Mississippi valley, just a bit too low of confidence for \nissuance of a Wind Advisory this afternoon.\n\nAs for rain, rapidly cooling temperatures aloft will lead to steep \nlow level lapse rates with moist conditions within much of the \nboundary layer. This should result in spotty open cellular \nconvection across the forecast area but, with shallow updrafts \nending below the -10C level, am not expecting thunder. Downdrafts \nlook to augment the risk for gusts at the surface due to the \naforementioned 35-40 knot winds present at 850mb.\n\nFreeze very likely Friday night into Saturday morning\n\nPost frontal surface high builds into KS Friday night with this \nsurface ridge extending northeast into WI. Clear skies and light \nwinds should lead to temperatures tumbling rapidly after sunset. \nGuidance probabilities point strongly to an areawide freeze (at \nleast 40/70/70% percent in 15.12z REFS/LREF/NBM). Since the locally \ndefined growing season is nearly over but still ongoing, the \nevent is within 48 hours, and confidence is at least 50%, the \ncriteria for a Freeze Watch has clearly been reached so have \nissued one for the entire CWA. For areas north of I-94, a \nkilling freeze with temps below 28 may (30-70%, highest in \nTaylor County) occur as well.",
